WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.850
Welcome to Level 1.

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:08.450
Before we touch robot hardware, we need to learn
how to give the computer clear instructions.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.500
Our robot controller is still a computer.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.550
It does not guess what we mean.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.050
C++ is one way to write instructions
clearly enough for the computer to follow.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.950
The code we type is called source code.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.400
A compiler checks that code and translates it.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.500
The result is a program the computer can actually run.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.700
This is a complete first C++ program.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:40.450
It may look strange today, but we only need
to recognize the major parts.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.900
The main function is where this program starts.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.400
The braces mark the group of instructions inside main.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.600
The cout line prints text to the terminal.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.000
The semicolon matters because many C++ statements
must end with one.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.850
Most of today is about the routine.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.250
Edit the file.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.250
Save it.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.450
Compile it.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.450
Run it.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.750
Then read the result or fix the error.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:15.450
To compile, we use G plus plus with the source file name
and output program name.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.000
Then we run the program and look for the output.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.150
Once Hello World works, change the printed message.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.750
A small edit proves that you are controlling the program,
not just copying it.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.050
Errors are normal feedback.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.900
If the compiler complains, check the nearby line.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:42.300
Missing quotes, braces, wrong folders, and semicolons
are common first fixes.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.850
For the skill check, write a program that prints three lines.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:54.100
Include main, braces, three cout lines,
a comment, and return zero.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.450
When you can explain the workflow
and run your edited program,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.850
you're ready for Lesson 2: structure,
variables, and clear code habits.
